title: 'pallet-revive: record no storage deposit for writes under Root'
doc:
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    Storage written by a call stack whose origin is `Root` no longer adds to the deposit a
    contract records.

    The pallet used to price such a write at `DepositPerByte` and `DepositPerChildTrieItem` and
    add it to `ContractInfo`, while settlement collected nothing, because Root has no account to
    charge. The contract recorded more deposit than it held, and a refund computed from that
    record paid out deposit other users had put down. A Root write now bumps the stored byte and
    item counts and leaves the recorded deposit alone.

    Storage a Root call writes still counts against the deposit limit at the list price. A Root
    call that writes more than its `storage_deposit_limit` allows fails with
    `StorageDepositLimitExhausted`. The call reports `Charge(0)` as its storage deposit, because
    nothing is collected, and reports the notional list price as its maximum. The base deposit of a
    contract a Root stack creates, and the code it uploads, cost nothing against that limit.

    Refunds change for everyone holding storage in a contract Root writes to. The pallet refunds
    a removal pro rata of the deposit the contract holds, over every unit it holds, and Root's
    units count toward that total without having added to the pool. A depositor who paid for `a`
    units of a contract that also holds `r` Root-written units gets back `a / (a + r)` of what
    they paid when removing them. The rest stays held on the contract. Before this
    change the same depositor got back everything they had paid, because a Root write added to
    the pool at list price as well as to the count.

    The pallet credits whoever removes Root-written units with a pro rata share of a pool they
    did not fund.
    Where one depositor paid 132 for two slots and Root then wrote fifty, an account that clears
    all fifty is credited 126 of that 132. With `Config::Deposit` left at the default `()` that
    account receives it. Under `PGasDeposit` the native part of a refund is capped at the
    account's own `NativeDepositOf` contribution, which is nothing here, so nothing is released
    and the call reports `Charge(0)` instead of a refund.

    A removal under Root takes at most its pro rata share out of the pool, and never takes the
    pool below the list price of the units that remain. While `DepositPerByte` and
    `DepositPerChildTrieItem` are unchanged, that floor is what the remaining depositors paid, so
    Root writing and then clearing its own units leaves them with everything they paid. After a
    decrease in either price the floor is below what they paid, and a Root round trip can leave
    the pool smaller than it was before. The difference stays held on the contract without being
    recorded.

    Deposit that leaves the record without being paid out stays held on the contract, and Root
    clearing storage a signed origin paid for strands it the same way. The record drops and the
    hold does not. Nothing releases it afterwards. A contract is deleted only by the call stack
    that created it, so one that has outlived that stack cannot be terminated, and its hold
    stays on it.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    There is no `Config` change and no storage migration. `ContractInfo` keeps its layout and
    recorded deposits stay valid.

    This release does not repair contracts that already record more deposit than they hold. A
    chain that has executed Root-originated storage writes keeps those records. The pallet
    bounds a refund from such a contract by the deposit it holds above its base deposit. A
    depositor removing storage is credited less than the record implies, rather than drawing on
    deposit somebody else put down.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: minor
- name: pallet-revive-fixtures
  bump: minor
